WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.325
Chapter 2: Context Engineering.

2
00:00:03.275 --> 00:00:10.275
Chapter 1 likened context to an Agent's "eyes" — an Agent can only make decisions based on what it sees.

3
00:00:10.275 --> 00:00:14.812
Designing and managing that information is called context engineering.

4
00:00:14.812 --> 00:00:22.037
Context is all the information the large language model (LLM) actually "sees" whenever you interact with it.

5
00:00:22.037 --> 00:00:34.925
It includes not only the conversation history, but also developer-written behavioral rules (the system prompt), descriptions of external capabilities available to the model (tool definitions), and other information.

6
00:00:34.925 --> 00:00:49.425
From the Harness perspective introduced in Chapter 1, context engineering is a core implementation of the Harness's "Context and Tools" layer: it determines what information the Agent sees at each decision point and how that information is structured.

7
00:00:49.425 --> 00:00:58.862
Well-designed context provides an efficient information-supply system, enabling the Agent to apply its general reasoning abilities fully to specific tasks.

8
00:00:58.862 --> 00:01:04.687
As illustrated in Figure 2-1: Overview of the Context Window Composition.

9
00:01:04.687 --> 00:01:09.562
Context: The Key Factor Determining an Agent's Capability Ceiling.

10
00:01:09.562 --> 00:01:17.562
Large language models achieve strong results on standardized benchmarks, but often disappoint in real-world business settings.

11
00:01:17.562 --> 00:01:28.950
That is because concrete tasks require background information—such as product architecture, business rules, and internal conventions—that a general-purpose model simply does not know.

12
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:32.887
Consider a highly capable engineer joining a new team.

13
00:01:32.887 --> 00:01:43.487
They may have deep theoretical knowledge and strong programming ability, but they do not yet understand the product architecture, business logic, technical debt, or team norms.

14
00:01:43.487 --> 00:01:53.750
If key architectural decisions are scattered across individual memories and the codebase is poorly documented, even an exceptional engineer will struggle to deliver value quickly.

15
00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.287
Today's AI Agents face the same problem.

16
00:01:57.287 --> 00:01:59.725
Consider a Coding Agent.

17
00:01:59.725 --> 00:02:07.975
Given the same instruction, "Help me fix this bug," the quality of the context the Agent receives determines whether it can complete the task:

18
00:02:07.975 --> 00:02:15.875
Code context: The codebase structure, module responsibilities, core data structures, and coding standards.

19
00:02:15.875 --> 00:02:24.225
Without this information, the Agent may produce code that is syntactically correct but inconsistent with the project's style or architecture.

20
00:02:24.225 --> 00:02:33.137
Process requirements: Git branching strategy, commit conventions, review process, and CI/CD requirements.

21
00:02:33.137 --> 00:02:38.975
Without this information, the Agent may commit untested code directly to the main branch.

22
00:02:38.975 --> 00:02:49.275
Environment configuration: Development setup, test database connection strings, test-environment deployment procedures, and API key management practices.

23
00:02:49.275 --> 00:02:55.300
Without this information, a fix that works locally may fail immediately in the test environment.

24
00:02:55.300 --> 00:03:03.687
These three categories—code, process, and environment—form the minimum context an Agent needs to work effectively.

25
00:03:03.687 --> 00:03:15.775
What enters the context here is an observation, description, or configuration of the Environment, not the Environment itself; the Environment remains the external object with which the Agent interacts.

26
00:03:15.775 --> 00:03:23.787
The model's inherent capability is only the foundation; context quality is what truly determines an Agent's capability ceiling.

27
00:03:23.787 --> 00:03:31.862
A moderately capable model with well-organized context can often outperform a stronger model operating with insufficient context.

28
00:03:31.862 --> 00:03:37.500
Context engineering is therefore central to building effective Agents with today's models.

29
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.400
It is not merely a matter of adding more text to a prompt.

30
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:48.837
It requires systematically designing, organizing, and providing the background knowledge the model needs to complete a task.

31
00:03:48.837 --> 00:03:54.875
Context engineering is not merely a technical problem, but also an organizational problem.

32
00:03:54.875 --> 00:04:07.787
In many teams, critical knowledge remains tacit: architectural decisions live in the memories of senior engineers, business rules are transmitted informally, and important context is buried in private chat logs.

33
00:04:07.787 --> 00:04:13.975
If the team itself is a poor information environment, even a strong AI Agent will be limited.

34
00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:20.827
Teams that work effectively in remote settings often also provide effective environments for AI Agents.

35
00:04:20.777 --> 00:04:30.040
Open-source projects such as the Linux kernel are instructive examples: developers distributed across the world have maintained the project for more than thirty years.

36
00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:35.990
This works because the project has a transparent, documentation-driven communication culture.

37
00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:43.440
Discussions are public, decisions are recorded, and newcomers can understand the evolution of the code by reading the history.

38
00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:51.352
The same working style naturally creates an AI-friendly environment: information is public, retrievable, and structured.

39
00:04:51.352 --> 00:04:56.252
Treat an AI Agent as a new team member each time it starts a task.

40
00:04:56.252 --> 00:05:03.915
With sufficient background, it can produce high-quality work; without that background, much of its intelligence is wasted.

41
00:05:03.915 --> 00:05:11.390
Building an AI-native team is therefore primarily a documentation effort, not merely a matter of deploying new tools.

42
00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:19.840
OpenAI researcher Jiayi Weng expressed this point clearly: "For both humans and models, the most important thing is Context.

43
00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:25.552
Reflecting on his own work, he noted: "My work at OpenAI isn't that difficult.

44
00:05:25.552 --> 00:05:29.690
If someone else had all my context, they could do it too.

45
00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:41.240
The same principle applies to Agents: the value an Agent delivers in a business often depends not on model size, but on the completeness and precision of the context provided at each decision point.

46
00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:53.002
Weng also observed that the central problem in teamwork is inconsistency of context, and that one reason AI cannot replace humans in the short term is that AI and humans do not share the same environment.

47
00:05:53.002 --> 00:06:01.540
Context engineering addresses exactly this problem: how to systematically deliver the structured background information an Agent needs to the model.

48
00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:07.902
ReAct is widely regarded as one of the foundational works on building Agents with large language models.

49
00:06:07.902 --> 00:06:15.077
The paper's opening sentence connects the relationships among the Agent, Environment, Context, and Action

50
00:06:15.077 --> 00:06:27.515
What matters most in this definition is not the symbols themselves, but that the Agent's next action depends on the complete interaction context accumulated up to the current point, not only on the input immediately in front of it.

51
00:06:27.515 --> 00:06:43.277
For an LLM Agent, user messages and tool execution results are observations returned by the Environment, while model replies and tool-call requests are actions taken by the Agent; these observations and actions alternate and accumulate into the interaction history.

52
00:06:43.277 --> 00:06:53.227
An actual API request also places the system prompt and tool definitions before this history, together forming the context the model receives in the current round.

53
00:06:53.227 --> 00:07:00.240
Because model APIs are stateless, the Agent framework must reconstruct sufficient context for every call.

54
00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:12.452
The most direct lossless approach is to include the complete message history so far; production systems may summarize and compress it, but must not silently discard information needed to determine the next action.

55
00:07:12.452 --> 00:07:25.315
All the context layouts, status bars, and compression techniques later in this chapter can be viewed as answers to one question: how can we provide the model with a sufficiently informative c_t at lower cost?

56
00:07:25.315 --> 00:07:31.377
The next question is how this contextual information is provided to the LLM at the technical level.

57
00:07:31.377 --> 00:07:36.440
How Agents Call LLMs: The API-Level Context Structure.

58
00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:42.077
This section uses OpenAI's Chat Completions API as a concrete example.

59
00:07:42.077 --> 00:07:55.690
Anthropic, Google, and other providers differ in details, but their Agent-facing APIs follow a similar pattern: each model call is constructed from a structured conversation history plus a set of available tool definitions.

60
00:07:55.690 --> 00:08:02.315
Understanding this structure is the foundation for the context engineering techniques discussed later in this chapter.

61
00:08:02.315 --> 00:08:04.640
The Four Message Roles.

62
00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:11.502
In Chat Completions-style APIs, the core input is a message list, usually named messages.

63
00:08:11.502 --> 00:08:17.202
Each message has a role field that tells the model how to interpret the message and where it came from:

64
00:08:17.356 --> 00:08:24.868
system: Developer-written instructions that define the Agent's identity, behavior, constraints, and workflow.

65
00:08:24.818 --> 00:08:28.468
The model treats this as a high-priority instruction.

66
00:08:28.468 --> 00:08:34.193
In most conversations, the system message appears once at the beginning of the message list.

67
00:08:34.193 --> 00:08:39.968
user: Input from the end user, representing the request the Agent needs to handle.

68
00:08:39.968 --> 00:08:46.818
assistant: Previous model outputs, including natural-language replies and tool call requests.

69
00:08:46.818 --> 00:08:55.456
In multi-turn interactions, these messages are included in later requests so the next stateless model call has access to the prior trajectory.

70
00:08:55.456 --> 00:09:00.356
tool: Results returned after the Agent framework executes a tool.

71
00:09:00.356 --> 00:09:10.506
Each tool result is linked to the corresponding tool call through tool_call_id, allowing the model to associate each result with the request that produced it.

72
00:09:10.506 --> 00:09:13.481
Tool definitions are not messages.

73
00:09:13.481 --> 00:09:21.843
They are provided in a separate tools field, which declares the tools available to the model and specifies the parameters each tool accepts.

74
00:09:21.843 --> 00:09:40.656
This is the same API request structure as the “five components of context” introduced in Chapter 1, classified from a different angle: the four system, user, assistant, and tool message roles correspond to the system prompt, user messages, assistant messages, and tool results, respectively.

75
00:09:40.656 --> 00:09:47.668
The remaining component—tool definitions—is passed through the top-level tools field rather than a message role.

76
00:09:47.668 --> 00:09:54.756
Thus, “four message roles + the tools field” exactly covers Chapter 1’s five context components.

77
00:09:54.756 --> 00:09:58.856
Single-Turn Request: The Simplest API Call.

78
00:09:58.856 --> 00:10:05.731
As illustrated in Figure 2-2: Request and Response Structure of a Single-Turn API Call.

79
00:10:05.731 --> 00:10:12.206
Start with the simplest case, without tool calls: the user asks, "Hello, who are you?

80
00:10:12.206 --> 00:10:17.818
This example uses a locally deployed Qwen3-0.6B model:

81
00:10:17.818 --> 00:10:27.318
Here is the structured JSON representation exchanged with the API, defining the model parameters, conversation history, and tool definitions.

82
00:10:27.318 --> 00:10:36.818
Here is the structured JSON representation exchanged with the API, defining the model parameters, conversation history, and tool definitions.

83
00:10:36.818 --> 00:10:46.006
This request contains only two messages: one system message containing rules written by the developer and one user message containing the user's input.

84
00:10:46.006 --> 00:10:49.693
The model returns an assistant message as the reply.

85
00:10:49.693 --> 00:10:59.506
This is the most basic LLM API interaction pattern: each call is stateless, so the request's message list must contain all the information the model needs.

86
00:10:59.506 --> 00:11:04.281
Multi-Turn Interaction with Tool Calls: The Core Loop of an Agent.

87
00:11:04.281 --> 00:11:09.268
Real Agent workflows are usually more complex than a single-turn Q&A.

88
00:11:09.268 --> 00:11:21.718
When a user asks, "What's the current time and weather in Vancouver?", the model cannot answer from its own knowledge (it does not know what time "now" is, much less the weather), so it must call external tools.

89
00:11:21.718 --> 00:11:27.268
The following example walks through each interaction between the Agent framework and the model.

90
00:11:27.268 --> 00:11:34.268
As illustrated in Figure 2-3: Complete Interaction Sequence for Two Model API Calls.

91
00:11:34.268 --> 00:11:41.231
The two calls in the figure both refer to calls to the model API, not to two tools being called sequentially.

92
00:11:41.231 --> 00:11:58.768
In this example, the timezone argument for get_current_time and the city and unit arguments for get_weather can all be determined up front; the weather service returns the city's latest weather itself and does not depend on the time tool's output, so the Agent framework can execute them in parallel.

93
00:11:58.768 --> 00:12:08.618
If a later tool's arguments must come from an earlier tool's result, the model must request that tool in a subsequent round, and the two tools must execute serially.

94
00:12:08.618 --> 00:12:13.343
First API call — Agent framework sends the initial request:

95
00:12:13.343 --> 00:12:22.843
Here is the structured JSON representation exchanged with the API, defining the model parameters, conversation history, and tool definitions.

96
00:12:22.996 --> 00:12:35.933
This tools list is static tool metadata the developer registered ahead of time: the tool names, descriptions and parameter schemas are written into the code and have nothing to do with what the user happens to be asking this time.

97
00:12:35.883 --> 00:12:49.821
Whether the user asks about the weather in Vancouver or asks the Agent to book a flight, the same list goes out; the example lists only the two relevant tools to keep the request short, whereas a real Agent often declares dozens of them at once.

98
00:12:49.821 --> 00:13:05.746
The Agent did not first split the user input into a "look up the time" subtask and a "look up the weather" subtask and then write the matching tool descriptions — that decomposition happens on the model's side, and it is precisely the tool_calls in the response below.

99
00:13:05.746 --> 00:13:10.258
Model returns a tool call request (not a final reply

100
00:13:10.258 --> 00:13:19.758
Here is the structured JSON representation exchanged with the API, defining the model parameters, conversation history, and tool definitions.

101
00:13:19.758 --> 00:13:23.158
The model does not answer the user's question yet.

102
00:13:23.158 --> 00:13:29.446
Instead, it returns two tool call requests: one for the current time and one for the weather.

103
00:13:29.446 --> 00:13:35.133
Because these requests are independent, the Agent framework can execute them in parallel.

104
00:13:35.133 --> 00:13:41.058
The model issues the call requests; the Agent framework performs the actual execution.

105
00:13:41.058 --> 00:13:53.646
This division of responsibility is central to Agent architecture: the model decides which tool to call and what arguments to pass, while the framework calls APIs, runs code, and returns the results.

106
00:13:53.646 --> 00:13:59.196
The Agent framework executes the tools and then initiates a second API call:

107
00:13:59.196 --> 00:14:14.396
After receiving the model's tool call requests, the Agent framework executes the two tools (for example, by calling a time API and a weather API), then sends the complete conversation history along with the tool execution results back to the model:

108
00:14:14.396 --> 00:14:23.896
Here is the structured JSON representation exchanged with the API, defining the model parameters, conversation history, and tool definitions.

109
00:14:23.896 --> 00:14:26.621
There are three key details here:

110
00:14:26.621 --> 00:14:38.521
The second request includes the full conversation history from the first request — the system message, the user message, the assistant message containing tool calls, and the newly added tool results.

111
00:14:38.521 --> 00:14:46.258
This illustrates the stateless nature of the API: the Agent framework must include the relevant history in every request.

112
00:14:46.258 --> 00:14:55.771
The first assistant message is inserted back into the message list verbatim — this gives the next model call access to the tool-call decisions made in the previous call.

113
00:14:55.771 --> 00:15:05.408
Tool messages are linked to their corresponding tool calls via tool_call_id — this tells the model which result belongs to which requested call.

114
00:15:05.408 --> 00:15:09.871
The model generates the final response based on the tool results:

115
00:15:10.036 --> 00:15:19.536
Here is the structured JSON representation exchanged with the API, defining the model parameters, conversation history, and tool definitions.

116
00:15:19.486 --> 00:15:30.173
This time, the model does not return tool_calls; it returns a text response because it judges that it has enough information to answer the user's question, and the Agent stops.

117
00:15:30.173 --> 00:15:39.686
This "request → tool call → execution → return results → next request" cycle is the API-level implementation of the ReAct loop introduced in Chapter 1.

118
00:15:39.686 --> 00:15:51.448
If the user wants more information (for example, by asking "What about Tokyo?"), the Agent framework appends the follow-up to the end of the conversation history and makes another model API call.

119
00:15:51.448 --> 00:16:00.123
The model begins returning tool_calls again, and the Agent framework executes them, sends back the results, and repeats the cycle.

120
00:16:00.123 --> 00:16:03.311
